> > On Wed, May 29, 2013 at 4:58 AM, Dennis Murphy <djm...@gmail.com> wrote:
> >> From what I've tried, ddply() strips the row names from the original
> >> data frame in the split phase.
> >
> > My point was that you don't need the original row names if you keep
> > the original data.frame together. There is no need to merge the
> > results back in, and hence no need to retain the row order or names.
> > Or maybe I'm wrong...
> >
> > Best,
> > Ista
> >
> >  my_data alternates A and B, but they
> >> are modeled first using the A subset and then the B subset. Ista's
> >> suggestion didn't retain the original row names when I tried it, nor
> >> did it when I tried my own version:
> >>
> >> library(plyr)
> >> mods <- dlply(my_data, "group", function(d) lm(y ~ x, data = d))
> >> lm_extract <- function(L)  data.frame(model.frame.lm(L),
> >>                                       fitted = fitted(L),
> >>                                       residuals = residuals(L))
> >> ddply(my_data, "group", lm_extract)
> >>
> >> The data frame that is output from group B is 'rbinded' to the output
> >> from A, and since the output from lm_extract() is not directly from
> >> the original data frame, it fails in the same way yours did. What
> >> ddply() is doing in this case is the equivalent of lapply ->
> >> do.call(rbind, output of lapply).
